[4418.758362, "i", "sed -n '1,240p' docs/legacy_api.md\n"]
[4418.758712, "o", "sed -n '1,240p' docs/legacy_api.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[4418.761797, "o", "# NumeriForge Legacy API Reference\r\n\r\n## Overview\r\n\r\n"]
[4418.761814, "o", "NumeriForge is a command-line scientific numerical experiment platform. It runs predefined experiment types, stores results, and provides comparison capabilities.\r\n\r\n## CLI Interface\r\n"]
[4418.761908, "o", "\r\n### `numeriforge run`\r\n\r\nExecutes a numerical experiment and stores the result.\r\n\r\n```\r\nnumeriforge run --experiment <name> [--param key=value ...] [--output-dir <dir>] [--config <file>] [--tag key=value ...]\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Flags:**\r\n- `--experiment <name>`: Required. The experiment type to run.\r\n- `--param key=value`: Experiment-specific parameters. Can be repeated.\r\n- `--output-dir <dir>`: Directory for storing results (default: `./results`).\r\n- `--config <file>`: INI-style configuration file.\r\n- `--tag key=value`: Attaches an arbitrary tag to the experiment result. Can be repeated. Tags are stored in result metadata as `tag_<key>`.\r\n\r\n**Output:** Prints experiment name, result ID, and all computed values to stdout.\r\n\r\n### `numeriforge compare`\r\n"]
[4418.761924, "o", "\r\nCompares two stored experiment results.\r\n\r\n"]
[4418.762008, "o", "```\r\nnumeriforge compare <id1> <id2> --store-dir <dir> [--mode exact|tolerance] [--abs-tol <val>] [--rel-tol <val>]\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Flags:**\r\n- `--store-dir <dir>`: Directory containing stored results.\r\n- `--mode exact|tolerance`: Comparison mode (default: `exact`).\r\n- `--abs-tol <val>`: Absolute tolerance (only for tolerance mode).\r\n- `--rel-tol <val>`: Relative tolerance (only for tolerance mode).\r\n\r\n**Exit codes:** 0 if results match, 1 if they differ.\r\n\r\n**Known issue:** The `exact` mode uses strict float equality which is unsuitable for scientific results with floating-point rounding. The `tolerance` mode is defined but not yet implemented.\r\n\r\n### `numeriforge list`\r\n\r\nLists stored experiment results.\r\n\r\n"]
[4418.762026, "o", "```\r\nnumeriforge list [--experiment <name>] [--store-dir <dir>] [--tag key=value ...]\r\n```\r\n"]
[4418.762098, "o", "\r\n### `numeriforge export`\r\n\r\nExports a result to JSON or CSV format.\r\n\r\n```\r\nnumeriforge export <id> --store-dir <dir> [--format json|csv]\r\n```\r\n\r\n**JSON export:** Includes a top-level `tags` object aggregating all `tag_<key>` metadata entries without the `tag_` prefix. The field is omitted when no tags are present.\r\n\r\n### `numeriforge verify`\r\n\r\nVerifies result integrity.\r\n\r\n"]
[4418.762113, "o", "```\r\nnumeriforge verify <id> --store-dir <dir> [--verbose]\r\n```\r\n"]
[4418.762134, "o", "\r\n## Experiment Types\r\n\r\n"]
[4418.762232, "o", "### Monte Carlo Experiments\r\n\r\n"]
[4418.762292, "o", "| Name | Description | Parameters |\r\n|------|-------------|------------|\r\n| `mc-estimate-pi` | Estimates pi via circle/square method | `samples` |\r\n| `mc-integral` | Numerical integration of sin(x)*exp(-x^2/2) | `samples`, `lower_bound`, `upper_bound` |\r\n| `mc-importance-sampling` | Importance sampling with Gaussian proposal | `samples`, `lower_bound`, `upper_bound`, `proposal_mean`, `proposal_std` |\r\n\r\n### Matrix Experiments\r\n\r\n| Name | Description | Parameters |\r\n|------|-------------|------------|\r\n| `matrix-lu-decompose` | LU decomposition with partial pivoting | `size`, `scale` |\r\n| `matrix-qr-decompose` | QR decomposition via modified Gram-Schmidt | `rows`, `cols`, `scale` |\r\n| `matrix-eigenvalue` | Dominant eigenvalue via power iteration | `size`, `scale`, `iterations` |\r\n\r\n### ODE Solver Experiments\r\n\r\n| Name | Description | Parameters |\r\n|------|-------------|------------|\r\n| `ode-euler` | Forward Euler method | `system_index`, `t_end`, `steps` |\r\n| `ode-rk4` | Classic 4th-order Runge-Kutta | `system"]
[4418.76231, "o", "_index`, `t_end`, `steps` |\r\n| `ode-system` | RK4 with noise injection | `system_index`, `t_end`, `steps`, `noise_amplitude` |\r\n\r\n**ODE Systems (by index):**\r\n- 0: Exponential decay (dy/dt = -0.5y)\r\n- 1: Harmonic oscillator\r\n- 2: Lorenz system (sigma=10, rho=28, beta=8/3)\r\n- 3: Van der Pol oscillator (mu=1)\r\n- 4: Stiff decay equation\r\n\r\n"]
[4418.762321, "o", "### Statistical Test Experiments\r\n\r\n| Name | Description | Parameters |\r\n|------|-------------|------------|\r\n"]
[4418.76242, "o", "| `stats-ttest` | Welch's two-sample t-test | `n1`, `n2`, `mean1`, `mean2`, `std1`, `std2` |\r\n| `stats-chi-squared` | Chi-squared goodness-of-fit | `n_categories`, `n_observations` |\r\n| `stats-ks-test` | Two-sample Kolmogorov-Smirnov test | `n1`, `n2`, `dist1_mean`, `dist1_std`, `dist2_mean`, `dist2_std` |\r\n\r\n## Result Storage Format\r\n\r\nResults are stored as JSON files in per-experiment subdirectories under the output directory:\r\n\r\n```\r\nresults/\r\n├── index.txt\r\n├── mc-estimate-pi/\r\n│   └── mc-estimate-pi_20240101_120000.json\r\n└── matrix-lu-decompose/\r\n    └── matrix-lu-decompose_20240101_120100.json\r\n```\r\n\r\nEach result JSON contains:\r\n- `id`: Unique identifier\r\n- `experiment_name`: Experiment type\r\n"]
[4418.762436, "o", "- `timestamp`: ISO 8601 timestamp\r\n- `parameters`: Input parameters\r\n- `values`: Computed numerical values\r\n"]
[4418.762525, "o", "- `labels`: Human-readable labels for values\r\n- `metadata`: Key-value pairs with method details\r\n\r\n**Known limitation:** Results do not include integrity checksums. There is no way to verify that stored values have not been corrupted or tampered with.\r\n\r\n## Configuration File Format\r\n\r\nINI-style configuration:\r\n\r\n```ini\r\nexperiment = mc-estimate-pi\r\noutput_dir = ./results\r\nparam.samples = 100000\r\n"]
[4418.76254, "o", "```\r\n\r\n## Known Issues\r\n\r\n"]
[4418.762615, "o", "1. **Non-reproducible results**: All stochastic experiments use a global random number generator seeded with the current time. Running the same experiment twice will produce different results. There is no `--seed` parameter.\r\n\r\n2. **Broken comparison**: The `exact` comparison mode uses strict float equality (`==`), which almost always reports mismatches for scientific computations due to floating-point rounding. The `tolerance` mode is defined in the code but not implemented.\r\n\r\n3. **No integrity verification**: Results are stored without checksums. The `verify` command is a stub.\r\n\r\n4. **No environment tracking**: There is no record of the runtime environment (Go version, OS, architecture, CPU features) that produced a result. This makes cross-platform reproducibility impossible to verify.\r\n"]
[4418.763103, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[4418.763168, "o", "root@n30009:/app# "]
[4419.041361, "i", "sed -n '1,240p' docs/reproducibility_spec.md\n"]
[4419.041781, "o", "sed -n '1,240p' docs/reproducibility_spec.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[4419.044834, "o", "# NumeriForge Reproducibility Specification v1.0\r\n\r\n## 1. Purpose\r\n\r\n"]
[4419.044931, "o", "This document defines the reproducibility guarantees that NumeriForge provides. It covers seed management for deterministic execution, result integrity via checksums, and tolerance-based comparison for scientific value matching.\r\n\r\n## 2. Seed Management\r\n\r\n### 2.1 Seed Parameter\r\n\r\nAll experiment backends that use random number generation must accept a `seed` parameter:\r\n\r\n- The CLI must accept a `--seed <integer>` flag.\r\n- When a seed is provided, the experiment must produce **bit-identical** results across runs on the same platform.\r\n- When no seed is provided, the system must generate a random seed and include it in the result metadata under the key `\"seed\"`.\r\n- The seed must be recorded in the result metadata regardless of how it was obtained.\r\n\r\n### 2.2 Isolated Random State\r\n\r\nEach experiment invocation uses its own isolated random state seeded with the provided or generated seed. Experiments do not share random state with each other or with a global source.\r\n\r\n### 2.3 Deterministic Guarantee\r\n\r\nGiven t"]
[4419.045015, "o", "he same seed and parameters:\r\n- Monte Carlo experiments must produce identical value sequences.\r\n- Matrix experiments that generate random matrices must produce identical matrices.\r\n- ODE solvers with noise injection must produce identical noise sequences.\r\n- Statistical test experiments must generate identical samples.\r\n\r\nODE solvers without noise (e.g., `ode-euler`, `ode-rk4`) are already deterministic since they depend only on the system definition and step parameters.\r\n\r\n## 3. Result Integrity\r\n\r\n### 3.1 Checksum Computation\r\n\r\nEvery stored result must include a SHA-256 checksum of the canonical value representation.\r\n\r\n**Canonical representation algorithm:**\r\n1. Take the `Values` float64 slice from the result.\r\n"]
[4419.045114, "o", "2. For each value, format it as `%.17e` (17 significant decimal digits in scientific notation). This ensures bit-level reproducibility of the text representation.\r\n3. Join all formatted values with newline characters (`\\n`).\r\n4. Compute SHA-256 of the resulting UTF-8 byte string.\r\n5. Encode the hash as lowercase hexadecimal.\r\n\r\n**Storage:**\r\n- The checksum must be stored in the `StoredResult` JSON under the key `\"checksum\"`.\r\n- The checksum must also be stored in the result metadata under the key `\"values_checksum\"`.\r\n\r\n### 3.2 Verify Command\r\n\r\n"]
[4419.045192, "o", "The `numeriforge verify` command must:\r\n1. Load the stored result by ID.\r\n2. Recompute the checksum from the stored values using the canonical algorithm.\r\n3. Compare with the stored checksum.\r\n4. Print `INTEGRITY_OK: <id>` and exit 0 if checksums match.\r\n5. Print `INTEGRITY_FAIL: <id>` and exit 1 if they differ.\r\n6. Print `NO_CHECKSUM: <id>` and exit 2 if the stored result has no checksum field.\r\n7. When `--verbose` is supplied, after the `INTEGRITY_OK` or `INTEGRITY_FAIL` line, also print:\r\n   - `Stored checksum: <hex>`\r\n   - `Computed checksum: <hex>`\r\n   Verbose mode does not change the exit code and does not apply to `NO_CHECKSUM`.\r\n\r\n## 4. Tolerance-Based Comparison\r\n\r\n### 4.1 Comparison Rules\r\n\r\nThe `tolerance` comparison mode must implement the following per-element comparison logic:\r\n\r\nFor each pair of values (a, b):\r\n\r\n1. **NaN handling**: If both are NaN, they match. If only one is NaN, they do not match.\r\n2. **Inf handling**: If both are +Inf, they match. If both are -Inf, they match. Mixed signs o"]
[4419.045268, "o", "r one finite and one infinite do not match.\r\n3. **Zero handling**: If both |a| and |b| are less than the absolute tolerance, they match.\r\n4. **General case**: The values match if **either** of these conditions holds:\r\n   - Absolute: `|a - b| <= absolute_tolerance`\r\n   - Relative: `|a - b| / max(|a|, |b|) <= relative_tolerance`\r\n\r\n### 4.2 Comparison Output\r\n\r\nThe comparison report for tolerance mode must include:\r\n- The tolerances used (absolute and relative).\r\n- Per-element match status for mismatched pairs.\r\n- For each mismatch: index, label, both values, absolute difference, and relative difference.\r\n- Overall match/mismatch summary.\r\n\r\n### 4.3 Default Tolerances\r\n\r\n- Default absolute tolerance: `1e-10`\r\n- Default relative tolerance: `1e-8`\r\n"]
[4419.045339, "o", "\r\n## 5. Backward Compatibility\r\n\r\nResults stored without checksums (pre-reproducibility) remain loadable. The verify command reports `NO_CHECKSUM` for such results rather than treating them as corrupt.\r\n"]
[4419.04584, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
